## Testing Rules

### Use WxtVitest + fakeBrowser

{% raw %}
```typescript
// vitest.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';
import { WxtVitest } from 'wxt/testing/vitest-plugin';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [WxtVitest()],
});
```
{% endraw %}

`WxtVitest()` (from `wxt/testing/vitest-plugin`) auto-polyfills `import.meta.env`, the `browser` global, and in-memory storage.
Reset state between tests:

{% raw %}
```typescript
import { fakeBrowser } from 'wxt/testing/fake-browser';
import { beforeEach, describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';

beforeEach(() => {
  fakeBrowser.reset(); // clears storage, listeners, mock tabs
});
```
{% endraw %}

### Test storage items

{% raw %}
```typescript
import { settings } from '@/utils/storage';

it('returns the fallback before anything is written', async () => {
  expect(await settings.getValue()).toEqual({ theme: 'light' });
});

it('persists an update', async () => {
  await settings.setValue({ theme: 'dark' });
  expect(await settings.getValue()).toEqual({ theme: 'dark' });
});
```
{% endraw %}

### Test message handlers

Register the background handlers, then drive them through `sendMessage`, asserting on the
response and any storage side-effects. Validate that handlers reject untrusted senders.

### Test content-script logic

Extract pure DOM/transform logic into `utils/` functions and unit-test those directly.
Use `@vitest/browser` or jsdom only for code that truly needs a DOM.

### Test UI surfaces

Keep surface logic in hooks/composables/`utils/` and test those with `fakeBrowser`. For a
rendered surface, use a DOM environment for that file only (`// @vitest-environment happy-dom`)
{% if variables.ui_framework == 'React' %}and `render(<App />)` from `@testing-library/react`;
{% elif variables.ui_framework == 'Vue' %}and `mount(App)` from `@vue/test-utils`;
{% elif variables.ui_framework == 'Svelte' %}and `render(App)` from `@testing-library/svelte`;
{% else %}and import the surface's `main.ts` after creating `<div id="app">`;
{% endif %}
assert on text and roles, and drive storage through the real `storage.defineItem` items.

### E2E with Playwright (real extension, Chromium)

Unit tests fake the browser; one or two E2E specs prove the built extension loads and its
surfaces work. Follow `.claude/patterns/{{ 'patterns/testing.md' | installed(stack) }}` section 7: `npm run test:e2e` builds
`.output/chrome-mv3` and runs Playwright with a persistent Chromium context
(`--load-extension`). The `extensionId` fixture comes from the service worker URL; open
surfaces at `chrome-extension://${extensionId}/popup.html` (also `options.html`,
`sidepanel.html`). Only Chromium loads unpacked extensions this way: Firefox and Safari
builds are checked manually.

## Coverage Targets

- Every message in the `ProtocolMap` has at least one handler test
- Every `storage.defineItem` has fallback + round-trip + migration tests
- Permission-gated paths assert the guard rejects when permission is absent
- Cross-browser branches (`import.meta.env.FIREFOX`, MV2/MV3) are both exercised
- An E2E smoke spec: the service worker starts and the popup renders

